    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Opponents of reparations often argue that such payments would be incredibly difficult to administer and could lead to significant economic harm, including inflation or an imbalance of power between groups. They also contend that \"canceling\" generations of debt through a formal policy is not the right way to achieve social justice, preferring instead systemic reforms like affirmative action or social spending. Furthermore, some critics believe that framing the issue strictly around \"individual harm\" harms White America's sense of involvement and responsibility in solving social problems. Historically, opponents frequently cite the historical integrity of slavery itself, arguing that restitution is morally impossible given the private nature of the institution at the time.",
